            accessStatus = "Municipal Airport & Freeman Army Airfield Museum",
            fullStory = "Established in 1942 as an advanced multi-engine flight training base, Freeman Field became the stage for one of the most pivotal civil rights victories in military history in April 1945.\n\nWhen the base commander issued Base Regulation 85-2 to enforce racial segregation at the officers' club—designating African American officers of the 477th Bombardment Group (the famed Tuskegee Airmen) as 'trainees' barred from the primary facility—dozens of Black officers entered the club in direct defiance of the order (61 were arrested over two days). When subsequently ordered to sign a statement endorsing the segregated policy, 101 Black officers refused. They were arrested, transported under armed guard, and faced potential court-martial. Their courageous, principled resistance caused national outrage and served as a direct catalyst for President Harry S. Truman's Executive Order 9981 desegregating the armed forces in 1948.",
            verifiedHistory = "In April 1945, officers of the 477th Bombardment Group (Medium) protested the segregated officers' club at Freeman Field. Sixty-one were arrested during a two-day protest on April 5-6, and on April 11, 101 officers who refused to sign Base Regulation 85-2 were arrested in quarters. Three officers faced a general court-martial in July 1945 for jostling an officer: Roger C. Terry was convicted, and the other two were acquitted. In 1995 the Air Force set aside Terry's conviction and removed letters of reprimand from the files of 15 of the 104 officers charged (the rest to be removed on request). When the base closed in 1948, most of it became Freeman Municipal Airport, with part used as an industrial park.",
            folkloreTradition = "Local aviation lore also tells of post-WWII secret test hangars where captured German Luftwaffe advanced aircraft (including Messerschmitt Me 262 jets) were evaluated before being transferred to Wright Field.",
            confidenceLevel = ConfidenceLevel.OFFICIAL_SOURCE.name,
            sources = "Indiana Historical Bureau Marker 'Freeman Field Mutiny'; National Archives Records of the Army Air Forces RG 18; James C. Warren, 'The Tuskegee Airmen Mutiny at Freeman Field' (1995).",
