            accessStatus = "Historic Marker / Active Aerospace & Museum Facility",
            fullStory = "In 1942, in the wake of Pearl Harbor, Republic Aviation opened a massive manufacturing plant near the Evansville municipal airport (the plant was finished in 1943) to produce the legendary P-47 Thunderbolt fighter plane—affectionately known as 'The Jug' for its rugged, bottle-like silhouette.\n\nWorking around the clock in three shifts, over 10,000 workers—more than half of whom were women ('Rosie the Riveters')—manufactured an astounding 6,242 P-47 Thunderbolts, representing nearly half of all P-47s built during the entire war. The Evansville-built Thunderbolts saw heavy combat in both the European and Pacific theaters, providing vital fighter escort for B-17 bombers and deadly ground-attack firepower during the Battle of the Bulge. The city's immense war production made Evansville a critical center of the American 'Arsenal of Democracy'.",
            verifiedHistory = "Republic Aviation opened its Evansville factory in 1942. The first Evansville-built P-47, 'The Hoosier Spirit', flew on September 19, 1942, and the plant was completed in August 1943. About 6,242 P-47s were built here by the P-47 Foundation's count (other sources say over 6,000 or over 6,600); the last left the plant on September 27, 1945. After the war the plant became a refrigeration plant, which closed in 2010. A state historical marker (installed 2023) stands at the Evansville Wartime Museum.",
            folkloreTradition = "Aviation lore held that the P-47s produced here were so stoutly built that pilots regularly flew home with missing cylinder heads, sheared wings, and tree branches stuck in the tail gear.",
