            fullStory = "Opened in 1938 (some accounts say it was dedicated in late 1937) as a Public Works Administration project under President Franklin D. Roosevelt, Lincoln Gardens was a New Deal public housing development built for African Americans in Evansville's historic Baptisttown neighborhood, on four city blocks cleared in 1937.\n\nDuring World War II, when Black servicemen stationing or transiting through Evansville were strictly barred from the local USO facilities due to racial segregation, the residents of Lincoln Gardens took action. They established their own welcoming USO club and lounge within the community center, providing hospitality, hot meals, music, and dignity to thousands of African American troops heading overseas. Today, the surviving complex houses the Evansville African American Museum, celebrating the rich culture, civil rights milestones, and perseverance of Southern Indiana's Black community.",
